      "content": "Thanks for that table, it really helps visualize the taxonomy they use. It's quite a diverse range of claims.\n\nI noticed in your table that some of the phenotypes listed are purely physical/physiological (like skin or circulatory systems), while others are specifically cognitive or psychological (like executive function or social intelligence). \n\nTo make this taxonomy more precise for the researcher, could you expand the section on the cognitive and psychological phenotypes? I'm specifically interested in the \"cognitive/behavioral\" side of their arguments. How do they typically map these same environmental stressors to more complex traits like IQ, temperament, or even predispositions toward specific types of social organization? I want to see the \"mapping\" they provide for the more controversial psychological claims.",
